FAIL:
- Q2: No explicit "Hamiltonian & Rydberg mapping" section heading or parameter regime table mapping Rydberg quantities (C₆, Ω, Δ, R_b) to model parameters (J, h_x, h_z) with typical experimental values. The convention dictionary (lines 93–114) partially fills this role, but there is no table of the form "Rydberg quantity → Model parameter → Typical value" as in XY_XXZ.qmd. The reader must reverse-engineer the mapping from inline equations.
- Q9: Line 122 says "quantum induced phase transition… manifest a phase transition introduced by the quantum fluctuation (ν ≈ 0.629) rather that classical thermal fluctuation (ν = 1 in 1d, ν = 0.5 in 2d with average field approximation)." This is confused and internally contradicted by the clarification box starting at line 127 which correctly states ν = 1 is the *quantum* critical exponent for the 1D quantum TFIM (from the 2D classical Ising universality class), not a "classical thermal fluctuation." The main text at line 122 says ν = 1 *is* the classical thermal fluctuation exponent, then the box says this interpretation is wrong. The main text was never corrected — the two statements directly contradict each other.
- Q9: Line 122 states "ν = 0.5 in 2d with average field approximation." The clarification box (line 136) says ν = 1/2 is exact at or above the upper critical dimension d_c = 3. The main text conflates "2D mean-field" with the upper-critical-dimension result, which is misleading.
- Q10: Line 89 writes the Rydberg Hamiltonian as $H_{\text{Ryd}} = \sum_{i<j} J_{ij} \sigma^z_i \sigma^z_j - h_x \sum_i \sigma^x_i - h_z \sum_i \sigma^z_i$ with $h_x = -\Omega/2$. But index.qmd @eq-tfim writes $H = \frac{\Omega}{2}\sum_i \sigma^x_i$. The sign convention difference ($h_x = -\Omega/2$ vs. coefficient $+\Omega/2$) is correctly explained in the convention dictionary, so this is consistent — however, the condensed-matter form in @thm-tfim-otoc uses $-J$ and $-h$ convention (line 23), while the Rydberg form has $+J_{ij}$. The dictionary at line 113 explains this via sublattice rotation only for bipartite lattices, but @thm-qma-complete-tfim (line 72) writes the QMA-completeness Hamiltonian using $-J \sum \sigma^z_i \sigma^z_j$ on a general graph $\langle i,j \rangle$, not necessarily bipartite. The mapping only works on bipartite lattices, so the QMA result for general graphs cannot be connected to the Rydberg AFM form via the stated sublattice rotation. This gap is not flagged.
- Q11: Line 80 states "for classical tensor-network methods (DMRG/MPS), representing this highly entangled state requires an exponentially growing bond dimension, limiting the simulable system size to N ≤ 20–30 for ED." This conflates ED (exact diagonalization, which is not a tensor-network method) with DMRG/MPS. ED is limited by Hilbert space dimension (exponential in N), not bond dimension. DMRG can handle much larger 1D systems (N ~ 100+). The stated limit "N ≤ 20–30" applies to ED but not to DMRG.
- Q12: The "Interesting transitions" section (lines 85–125) is skeletal. It lists two transitions (checkerboard, quantum-induced QPT) with bullet-point observables and parameter settings but no derivation, no phase diagram, and no discussion of the mechanism connecting Rydberg parameters to the QPT. An experimentalist would not be able to reconstruct the mapping or understand *why* $R_b/a = 1.15$ is the relevant regime from what is written.
PRIORITY FIX: Q9 — line 122 directly contradicts the clarification box at line 127. The main-text critical exponent discussion is factually incorrect as written and must be harmonized with the (correct) box.

