# Cross-File Issues

1. **Notation: $n_i$ ambiguity.** Index.qmd (line 30) defines $n_i = |r_i\rangle\langle r_i|$ as a Rydberg projector with eigenvalues $\{0,1\}$. Boson-Hubbard.qmd (line 22) uses $\hat{n}_i = \hat{n}_{i\uparrow} + \hat{n}_{i\downarrow}$ with eigenvalues $\{0,1,2\}$. Fermi-Hubbard.qmd uses $n^d_i$ (line 184) for electron density. These are all different operators denoted by similar symbols. The Boson-Hubbard and Fermi-Hubbard pages operate in their own operator algebras (Schwinger bosons, slave spins) where $n_i$ takes different meanings, but no cross-reference to the index.qmd notation table is made.

2. **$S_i^+$ definition consistency.** Index.qmd (line 76) uses $S_i^+ S_j^-$ without explicit definition ($S^+ = S^x + iS^y$, spin-$\frac{1}{2}$ operators). XY_XXZ.qmd (line 23) uses the same convention. Boson-Hubbard.qmd (line 141) defines $\hat{S}_i^+ = \hat{a}_{i,\uparrow}^\dagger \hat{a}_{i,\downarrow}$ (Schwinger boson), which is compatible. Fermi-Hubbard.qmd (line 77) defines $\hat{S}_i^+ = c_{i\uparrow}^\dagger c_{i\downarrow}$ (electron operators). These are consistent in meaning but the *operator algebras* differ. No confusion arises because each page clearly specifies its operators.

3. **Systematic absence of parameter regime tables.** Only XY_XXZ.qmd and topological.qmd (SSH section only) include parameter regime tables. TFIM.qmd, ferm_Hubbard.qmd, Boson-Hubbard.qmd, honeycomb.qmd, puccd.qmd, dynamics.qmd, density_wave.qmd, LGT.qmd, and optimization.qmd all lack them. This is the single most pervasive structural deficiency across the catalog.

